Language: en

00:00:04.967 --> 00:00:06.300
JELENA MIJUSKOVIC: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.678
I'm Jelena.

00:00:07.678 --> 00:00:08.720
DEOK FILHO: And I'm Deok.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.768
And we're both customer
engineers at Google Cloud.

00:00:11.768 --> 00:00:14.060
JELENA MIJUSKOVIC: Welcome
back to the technical series

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.210
for startups, where we're
creating a series of videos

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:21.300
for technical enablement to
help startups to start, build,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.110
and grow their businesses
successfully and sustainably

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.283
in Google Cloud.

00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:26.700
DEOK FILHO: In the
previous video,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.840
we deep dived into automating
your development lifecycle

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.330
with CI and CD pipelines.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.360
And now, we'll talk about how
to improve the performance

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.370
and reliability of
your applications

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.645
with Cloud operations.

00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:40.020
JELENA MIJUSKOVIC:
In this video,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.680
we're going to
cover introduction

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.050
to the Cloud operation
suite, formerly known

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.580
as Tech Driver, Operation tools
as means to have observability

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.770
over your Google
Cloud resources,

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.600
application
performance management

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.300
tools to improve
performance and reliability

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.990
of your applications.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.320
Finally, we'll share a
customer success story

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.980
to demonstrate the value of the
Google Cloud Operation Suite.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.510
If you've ever worked with
on-premises environment,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.270
you know that, if your
application becomes

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.110
unresponsive, somebody could
walk in, physically check why,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.550
and resolve the issue.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.170
In the cloud, though,
how do you know

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.240
what's happening with
your server, database,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.530
or application?

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.900
The answer is simple,
with the tools that we

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.520
will show you in this video.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.520
Capturing of telemetry,
login, and trace data

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.480
is done from the hardware layer
and app for Google products.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.900
From these products, signal
data flows into Cloud Operation

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.700
tools, where it
can be visualized

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.820
in dashboards and through
the metrics explorer.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.910
Automated and custom logs
can be ingested and analyzed

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.590
in the log viewer.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.050
Services can be
monitored for compliance

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.880
with the service-level
objectives.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.280
And error budgets
can be tracked.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.100
Health checks can be used
to check uptime and latency

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.740
for external-facing
sites and services.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.100
And running applications can
be debugged and profiled.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.200
When incident is
secure, single data

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.270
can generate
automated alerts that

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.840
can notify personnel through
various notification channels.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.750
Error reporting can help
operation and developer teams

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.060
to spot, count,
and analyze crashes

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.040
in your cloud-based services.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.920
Finally, all the visualization
and analysis tools

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.920
can help to troubleshoot what is
happening in your Google Cloud

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.490
environment.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.610
Let's start first with
exploring the products that

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.520
tend to be of interest
for the operation folks.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.340
Cloud Logging provides a
centralized Cloud Logging

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.790
system that you can use
to store, search, analyze,

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:35.900
monitor, and alert log data and
events from all Google Cloud

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.510
resources but also
to collect data

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.840
from multicloud environments
with Anthos integration.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.940
Cloud Logging also
provides integration

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:48.170
into third-party systems through
the API and Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.120
The export
functionality allows you

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.790
to perform aggregated
analytics in BigQuery

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.670
and store your logs in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.440
As you can see from
this quick demo,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.380
with features like histogram,
Logs Field Explorer,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.310
you can quickly gain insights
into trends across your logs.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.250
Error reporting provides
insights into your errors

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.670
by collecting and grouping
your application errors.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.740
There are three
key log categories,

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:16.520
audit logs, agent
logs, and network logs.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.140
Audit logs help answer the
question, who did what,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.490
where, and when?

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.510
And those are admin activity
logs, data access logs,

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.630
system event, and data
transparency logs.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.660
Agent logs use a Google
customized and packaged Fluentd

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.680
agent that can be installed
on any Google Cloud or AWS VM

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.850
instance and ingest logs
from Google Cloud or AWS EC2

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.510
instance.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.160
Finally, network logs provide
in-depth network telemetry

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.970
with PPC flow logs,
firewall rules, NAT gateway,

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:48.830
and load balancer types.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.140
Cloud Monitoring allows
you to collect metrics

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.560
from multicloud and hybrid
infrastructure in real time.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.390
By using cloud
monitoring, you can

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.470
answer important questions like,
what is the load on my service?

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:03.260
Is my website
responding correctly?

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.180
Is my service performing well?

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.790
To understand the
current load on a service

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.160
or to view performance
data of your service

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.940
for the past month, use the
charts and dashboard tools.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:15.860
If you're interested
in being notified

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.050
if your deployed
service isn't accessible

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:21.560
or responding correctly, then
configure an uptime check

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.870
and attach an alerting policy.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.510
This is an example of
Metrics Explorer, where

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.600
you can explore metric
lists for all GCP services

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.850
and add them to
different chart types.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:34.610
Monitoring has many
predefined metric types

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.590
and monitored
resources available.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:39.320
But you can also
create custom metrics.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:42.110
If you don't want to save the
chart to a custom dashboard

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.450
but you want to keep a
reference to the chart,

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.790
then save the chart URL.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:49.550
Dashboards are one way for
you to view and analyze

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.290
metric data.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.080
The Cloud Console provides
15 predefined dashboards

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.810
that require no setup
or configuration.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.850
Your Google Cloud
project resources

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:01.350
determine the dashboards
available for you.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:04.220
For example, if your project
contains Compute Engine

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.310
instances, then your
project includes dashboards

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:10.010
for VM instances and for disks.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:11.310
DEOK FILHO: Hey, everyone.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.860
The next step is
for us to check out

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:17.570
some tools to help you manage
your application's performance.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.850
At the heart of measuring
application performance

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:23.060
in Google Cloud
lies Cloud Debugger.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.520
It lets you inspect the state
of a running application

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.190
in real time without
stopping your application

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:31.190
or slowing it down,
all at no cost.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:34.070
With Cloud Debugger, you can
examine your code's function

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.700
and performance under actual
production conditions.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.860
How convenient is that?

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.170
In addition to
real-time app debugging,

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:43.970
Cloud Debugger
allows you to capture

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:47.570
the state of your application
in production at a specific line

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.340
location with snapshots.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.470
You can see this on the right.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:54.320
I first set up a
snapshot at line 73.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.270
You can see, in the
snapshot column,

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.160
that it's waiting for a change.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.430
I then go to my application
and provoke a state change

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.590
by clicking the
Add to Cart button.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:07.190
You can immediately see the
state capture by the snapshot,

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:11.630
specifically, that the cost
ID variable, set at line 73,

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:14.300
has the value undefined.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:17.660
Moving on from snapshots,
you can also use log points

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.270
to inject a new logging
statement on demand

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.990
at a specific line location,
as well as capture a snapshot

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.760
or write a log
point message only

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.090
when you need it using a simple
conditional expression written

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.160
in your application's language.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:33.320
The ease of use
of Cloud Debugger

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.240
is further extended
by its integration

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:39.470
with existing developer
workflows and popular IDEs.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:42.050
You can easily collaborate
with other teams

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.240
by sharing your debug session.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.480
In this demo, I can simply
share my console URL

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:48.560
with a team member.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.070
And they'll be able to see
the snapshot I created.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:55.130
Debugger also knows how to
display the correct version

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.560
of your source code
because it easily

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:59.330
integrates with
the version control

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:02.870
systems such as cloud
source repositories, GitHub,

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.630
Bitbucket, or GitLab.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:07.820
Now that we've seen
how easy and convenient

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.340
it is to work with
Debugger, let me tell you

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.200
what Cloud Trace is.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.230
Based on the tools that Google
itself uses on its production

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:19.280
services, Cloud Trace is a
tracing system that collects,

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.190
analyzes, and
displays latency data

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:25.090
from your distributed
applications and displays

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.030
that in the Google
Cloud console.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.940
Cloud Trace can capture traces
from applications deployed

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:35.020
on App Engine, Compute Engine
VMs, and Kubernetes Engine

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:36.040
containers.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.310
It generates performance
insights near real time

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:43.430
and automatically generates
in-depth latency reports.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:46.720
Finally, Cloud Trace
also continuously gathers

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:49.750
and analyzes trace data
to automatically identify

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:53.080
recent changes to your
application's performance.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.050
Let me show you a quick
demo of Cloud Trace.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:59.590
First, when opening Cloud Trace
in the Google Cloud console,

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:02.560
I immediately see an
overview of previous traces

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.960
and report of latency.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.900
Let's navigate to
the trace list page.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.730
I'll create some
filters to see calls

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:11.980
to a currency service
of my application

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:13.900
that are only
getting get requests

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:18.130
which took at least 1,000
milliseconds from request

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:20.020
to response.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.090
I then select a
trace from the graph.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:25.060
And below, in the
trace waterfall view,

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.420
I can explore the spans,
which are suboperations

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.580
of this particular trace.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.370
Finally, under
the Details panel,

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:36.880
I select to View Logs, which
takes me to the Cloud Logging

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:40.900
page, where I can see detailed
log entries associated

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:44.920
with the request that
initiated this trace.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.320
In summary, this
allows you to see

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:51.520
how requests propagate through
services in separate projects

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.000
and helps you to identify
sources of poor performance

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.370
across your entire stack.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:02.920
On to error reporting, error
reporting counts, analyzes,

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:06.850
and aggregates the crashes in
your running cloud services.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:11.230
A dedicated view shows the
error details, time chart,

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:15.190
occurrences, affected
user count, first

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:19.990
and last seen dates, and a
cleaned exception stack trace.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:23.470
You can opt in to receive
email and mobile alerts

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:24.920
on new errors.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:27.340
Finally, the last
major component

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.450
of measuring your application's
performance is Cloud Profiler.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:34.450
Poorly performing code
increases the latency and cost

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:38.020
of applications in web services
every day without anyone

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:40.360
knowing or doing
anything about it.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.120
To solve it, Cloud
Profiler continuously

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:46.570
analyzes the performance
of CPU-intensive and

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:49.930
memory-intensive functions
across an application

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:53.860
Cloud Profiler does this by
using statistical techniques

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.590
and extremely low-impact
instrumentation

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:59.890
that runs across all production
application instances

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:05.200
to provide a complete CPU and
heat picture of an application

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:06.700
without slowing it down.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:09.160
To set up Cloud
profiler, you first

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.650
include a language-specific
profiling agent

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.370
in your application.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:17.530
Whether it is running in GC
EVMs, App Engine, or GKE,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:21.700
the agent then collects
profiling data as the app runs.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.890
As you can see from
the image on the right,

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:27.280
Cloud Profiler presents the
call hierarchy and resource

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.110
consumption of the
relevant function

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.480
and an interactive
flame graph that

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:34.630
helps developers understand
which paths consume the most

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:37.480
resources and the different ways
in which their code is actually

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:38.350
called.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.500
Now that you've seen many
operations in application

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.750
performance
management tools, how

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:46.720
should you use them to bring
your ideas to market faster

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.490
and to be more responsive
to your customers?

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:53.410
Well, Google's Site Reliability
Engineering, or SRE,

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:55.030
is the answer.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.990
SRE is how operations at
Google adopted DevOps.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:03.190
The five SRE design principles
redefine reliability,

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:08.410
reduce oil, balance speed
and risk, make small changes

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:10.750
and create the
right culture, help

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:13.070
you accomplish these goals.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.860
The tools we introduced
were designed

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.640
based on Google's own operations
experience and development

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:20.380
of SRE practices.

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:24.010
A key area of practice
for SRE is monitoring.

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:27.430
Cloud Monitoring, which pulls
data from multiple sources,

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.380
not only gives you
the visibility you

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.690
need to quickly
determine service health

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:35.680
and diagnose your applications
when things go wrong, but also

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:38.950
addresses the fundamental
monitoring needs of SRE,

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:42.220
with components such
as SLO monitoring,

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:46.270
dashboards, and its analytics
capabilities through default

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:48.310
and custom metrics.

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:51.670
Let me tell you about a
customer success story.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:53.530
Krikey is a gaming
company, which

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:56.680
aims to bring the excitement
of interacting with characters

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:58.930
through augmented
reality by leveraging

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:02.710
stories that are rarely
told in mainstream gaming.

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:06.070
With four separate games,
all requiring multiple video

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:08.710
feeds, they rely
on Cloud Operations

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:12.400
to monitor, troubleshoot, and
improve their application's

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:13.630
performance.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:16.630
In particular, the Cloud
Monitoring dashboard

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:20.200
gives them deep visibility
into latency, downtime,

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:23.170
and the overall health
of their applications.

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:27.520
Here is a joint quote
from Krikey's CEO and CTO.

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.200
"Having one integrated view of
alerts, SLOs, events, and logs

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:35.740
via the dashboard, which
allows us to quickly identify

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:38.980
which container has the
problem, fix the container,

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.482
and redeploy."

00:12:40.482 --> 00:12:42.190
JELENA MIJUSKOVIC: In
this episode today,

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:44.260
you got to see the
fundamental components

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.690
in making your
application perform better

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:50.800
with a consistent and effortless
observability and monitoring

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.550
of your resources.

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:56.110
Thank you for joining us on this
episode about Cloud Operations.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:58.930
To continue exploring,
